#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""tsx-setupd: the on-panel first-boot setup page (see the "Setup page" docs
of the board repository).

It always runs in the default runlevel, as the UNPRIVILEGED "tsx-setup"
system user, not as root. tsx-mqtt and tsx-esphome differ: this daemon
parses HTTP from the LAN before any login has happened, so it gets no
privilege at all. It serves a small, dark, touch-friendly page (docs/setup.md
of tsx-linux-common):

  * On the panel itself, over loopback (http://127.0.0.1:PORT/setup).
    kiosk-session loads this page instead of Home Assistant when no
    KIOSK_URL is configured, or for one ~15 minute window after
    `tsx-config setup` or the overlay "Setup" entry. Loopback never needs a
    login.
  * From a laptop or phone on the LAN (http://<panel-ip>:PORT/setup), with
    one of two logins:
      - "Log in with Home Assistant" (login_available() below): when the
        panel knows the URL of its Home Assistant (panel.conf HA_URL, else
        the origin of KIOSK_URL), the page is always on. A visitor logs in
        at that Home Assistant (its OAuth / IndieAuth flow, client_id = the
        origin of this page). tsx-setupd exchanges the code for tokens, asks
        the websocket API of Home Assistant if the user is an
        administrator, revokes the refresh token at once and keeps no token.
        Only an administrator gets a session. setup_ha_login.py (in
        ../lib/tsx) has the Home Assistant side.
      - The pairing code (pairing_open() below): while the panel is
        unconfigured or during that same ~15 minute window. It needs the
        code that the screen of the panel shows. The daemon sends the code
        to no LAN client any other way. A correct code gives a session for
        the rest of the window.
    When neither is possible, the process does not even listen on a
    LAN-reachable address (ServerManager below rebinds to loopback only).
    It does not just refuse at the HTTP layer.

Sessions (the Sessions class): the cookie "tsxsetup" (HttpOnly,
SameSite=Strict, Path=/setup) holds a random token. The server keeps the
list of sessions in memory, with an expiry (TSX_SETUP_SESSION seconds after
the login, TSX_SETUP_IDLE seconds after the last request, the end of the
window for a pairing session). A logout or a revoke removes the session from
the list at once. A restart of tsx-setupd ends all sessions.

CSRF: every POST must have the Content-Type application/json, an Origin
header (when the browser sends one) of this host, and the header
X-TSX-CSRF with the CSRF token of the session (loopback: the token of this
process). The state API and the cookie "tsxcsrf" (not HttpOnly, so the page
script can read it) give the token to the page. /setup/api/pair is the one
POST without a session: it has the Content-Type and Origin checks and its
rate limit. The login start and the return from Home Assistant have their
own rate limits per address.

The page has the fields of the base system: the page URL, the panel name,
the time zone, the display, the root login and the updates. Other packages add
their fields with a plugin file in /usr/local/share/tsx/setup.d (see "plugins"
below). The Home Assistant fields (the HA URL and login, voice, MQTT) are the
plugin of tsx-ha. Without it, this page never names Home Assistant.

Every privileged action goes through tsx-setup-helper. These actions are:
read or write panel.conf (mode 600, owned by root), apply it, restart the
kiosk, and change the root password. tsx-setup-helper is a small root daemon
with a fixed, line-based command set
(rootfs/overlay/usr/local/sbin/tsx-setup-helper). This process never runs a
shell that is built from form input. The one check that needs no privilege
(`tsx-config validate KEY VALUE`, with the exact same regex table as
`tsx-config set`) runs directly, because it touches no file.

The daemon never echoes secrets (HA_TOKEN, MQTT_PASSWORD,
SSH_AUTHORIZED_KEY, the root password) back to a client, and never writes
them to its own log. The state API reports only whether such a key is
already set, never its value. It reuses the masking of `tsx-config show`
and does not reimplement it. The daemon never logs anything about a POST
body.

A save writes only the fields that the user changed. The state API gives the
page the revision of panel.conf (tsx-setup-helper "rev": a salted hash of the
content of the file). The page keeps the values that it showed at load time,
and a save sends only the fields with a different value, plus that revision.
The server sets or removes only the keys that the page sent. If panel.conf
changed after the page loaded (tsx-config, Home Assistant, another browser),
the server refuses the save, changes nothing and tells the user to reload the
page. A save that changes nothing does not run tsx-config apply. The page
fills its fields once, at load time. Its refresh every 20 s reads only
/setup/api/status (the pairing code and the revision) and never changes a
field.

On a panel with no light sensor (ALS=no in /run/tsx/hw.conf from tsx-hw), the
page shows the light sensor settings (AUTO_BRIGHTNESS and ALS_SCALE) as not
available. A submit then leaves them as they are. A plugin can report more
settings in the same way (tsx-ha: no microphone and no Bluetooth module).

A panel can run with no browser kiosk. A native panel app then owns the
screen, and the package tsx-kiosk is not installed. The setup.conf key
TSX_SETUP_KIOSK (auto, on or off) sets the mode. With "auto" (the default),
the mode is "on" when /etc/kiosk.conf exists. With the mode "off":

  * The page has no page URL card, and a save does not need KIOSK_URL. The
    page also hides the fields that only a service of tsx-kiosk reads:
    KIOSK_URL, KIOSK_DARK and BLANK_TIMEOUT (tsx-idled). A plugin names its own such
    keys in KIOSK_ONLY_KEYS. A save leaves these keys as they are.
  * The panel is "configured" when panel.conf has at least one key. While
    it is not configured, a save always sends TZ_NAME, so the first save
    makes panel.conf and closes the LAN listener.
  * A save restarts no kiosk.
  * The screen of the panel is not a browser. So the pairing code goes to
    the native app in the file screen.json (see write_screen below).

screen.json: while pairing_open() is true, this process writes the file
STATE_DIR/screen.json (mode 600, one JSON object, atomic: a temporary file
and a rename) and writes it again at least every 5 s (every REFRESH_S). When
this process is a member of the group tsx-setup-screen, the file gets that
group and mode 640: the panel app runs as a user of that group
(docs/security.md) and shows the code. It removes the file when the LAN
window closes and when the process ends:

  {"code": "123456", "port": 8080, "path": "/setup",
   "addresses": ["192.0.2.10"], "remaining": 840, "uptime": 1234}

addresses are the IPv4 addresses of the panel that are not loopback.
remaining is the number of seconds until the code expires. uptime is the
integer seconds of /proc/uptime at the write. A native app shows the text
only while the file exists and its uptime is less than 30 s old. Loopback
clients get the code from the state API too, as in the kiosk mode.

A save (handle_submit) restarts the kiosk only when the setup page is on the
screen (a loopback request or the open window) or when the save changed a
key that the kiosk reads at its start (KIOSK_URL, KIOSK_DARK, HA_LOGIN_METHOD,
HA_TOKEN). A remote administrator who changes MQTT does not reload the
screen of the panel.

The Panel section of the page has live controls (/setup/api/panel): the
screen, the backlight, the volume, the screen-off and dim times, and the
LED bar, the key LEDs or the lights of the panel app. It uses the backends
of the overlay and of the Home Assistant entities: tsx-panelctl (through the
helper commands "panel-state" and "panel") for the live values, and
tsx-config (set and apply) for the times. A control shows only when the
panel has the part (`tsx-panelctl state`).

Env overrides for host tests (tests/test-setup.sh): TSX_SETUP_PORT,
TSX_SETUP_CONF, TSX_HW_CONF, TSX_RUN_DIR, TSX_CONFIG_BIN, TSX_SETUP_HELPER_REQ,
TSX_SETUP_HELPER_RESP, TSX_ZONEINFO_DIR, TSX_SETUP_NO_ZEROCONF=1,
TSX_SETUP_PLUGIN_DIR (default /usr/local/share/tsx/setup.d), TSX_KIOSK_CONF,
TSX_SETUP_KIOSK (replaces the setup.conf key), TSX_SETUP_STATE_DIR (default
/run/tsx-setup), TSX_SETUP_SESSION and TSX_SETUP_IDLE (replace the setup.conf
keys; tests/test-setup-login.sh).
"""
import hmac
import importlib.util
import json
import os
import re
import secrets
import select
import signal
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
import threading
import time
import urllib.parse
from http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, ThreadingHTTPServer

# The Home Assistant side of the login: ../lib/tsx/setup_ha_login.py next to
# the real file of this script (the same layout in the repository).
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)), "..", "lib", "tsx"))
import setup_ha_login  # noqa: E402

TSX_CONFIG_BIN = os.environ.get("TSX_CONFIG_BIN", "tsx-config")
ZONEINFO_DIR = os.environ.get("TSX_ZONEINFO_DIR", "/usr/share/zoneinfo")
RUN_DIR = os.environ.get("TSX_RUN_DIR", "/run/tsx")
HW_CONF_PATH = os.environ.get("TSX_HW_CONF", os.path.join(RUN_DIR, "hw.conf"))
CONF_PATH = os.environ.get("TSX_SETUP_CONF", "/etc/tsx/setup.conf")
KIOSK_CONF_PATH = os.environ.get("TSX_KIOSK_CONF", "/etc/kiosk.conf")
NO_ZEROCONF = os.environ.get("TSX_SETUP_NO_ZEROCONF", "0") == "1"
PLUGIN_DIR = os.environ.get("TSX_SETUP_PLUGIN_DIR", "/usr/local/share/tsx/setup.d")
STATE_DIR = os.environ.get("TSX_SETUP_STATE_DIR", "/run/tsx-setup")


def load_conf(path):
    cfg = {}
    try:
        with open(path, "r", errors="replace") as f:
            for line in f:
                line = line.strip()
                if not line or line.startswith("#") or "=" not in line:
                    continue
                k, v = line.split("=", 1)
                k, v = k.strip(), v.strip()
                if len(v) >= 2 and v[0] == v[-1] and v[0] in "\"'":
                    v = v[1:-1]
                cfg[k] = v
    except OSError:
        pass
    return cfg


_conf = load_conf(CONF_PATH)
try:
    PORT = int(_conf.get("TSX_SETUP_PORT") or "8080")
except ValueError:
    PORT = 8080
LAN_ON = (_conf.get("TSX_SETUP_LAN") or "on").strip().lower() != "off"
try:
    WINDOW = int(_conf.get("TSX_SETUP_WINDOW") or "900")
except ValueError:
    WINDOW = 900
HA_LOGIN = (_conf.get("TSX_SETUP_HA_LOGIN") or "on").strip().lower() != "off"


def _conf_seconds(key, default, low, high):
    """A time of setup.conf (the environment wins: host tests)."""
    try:
        v = int(os.environ.get(key) or _conf.get(key) or default)
    except ValueError:
        v = default
    return max(low, min(high, v))


SESSION_S = _conf_seconds("TSX_SETUP_SESSION", 28800, 5, 7 * 86400)
IDLE_S = _conf_seconds("TSX_SETUP_IDLE", 3600, 5, 7 * 86400)
KIOSK_MODE = (os.environ.get("TSX_SETUP_KIOSK") or _conf.get("TSX_SETUP_KIOSK") or "auto").strip().lower()
if KIOSK_MODE not in ("auto", "on", "off"):
    KIOSK_MODE = "auto"
# the keys that only a service of tsx-kiosk reads (tsx-idled reads BLANK_TIMEOUT)
BASE_KIOSK_ONLY_KEYS = ("KIOSK_URL", "KIOSK_DARK", "BLANK_TIMEOUT")


def kiosk_enabled():
    """True when the panel runs the browser kiosk: TSX_SETUP_KIOSK=on, or
    "auto" and /etc/kiosk.conf (the file of tsx-kiosk) exists."""
    if KIOSK_MODE == "auto":
        return os.path.exists(KIOSK_CONF_PATH)
    return KIOSK_MODE == "on"


# ---- tsx-config validate (needs no privilege: key_known and val_ok touch no
# file, so this runs directly and unprivileged. A pure yes/no check does not
# need root) ----------------------------------------------------------------

def _run(argv, **kw):
    kw.setdefault("capture_output", True)
    kw.setdefault("timeout", 10)
    try:
        return subprocess.run(argv, **kw)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as e:
        class _Fake:
            returncode = 1
            stdout = b""
            stderr = str(e).encode()
        return _Fake()


def tcfg_validate(key, val):
    if "\n" in val or "\r" in val:
        return False
    return _run([TSX_CONFIG_BIN, "validate", key, val]).returncode == 0


# ---- tsx-setup-helper: the ONLY way this (unprivileged) process reads or
# writes panel.conf, applies it, restarts the kiosk, or changes the root
# password. rootfs/overlay/usr/local/sbin/tsx-setup-helper, its own root
# daemon, is the one process with that privilege. Every request here is one
# line, shaped like argv ("CMD KEY VALUE"), never a shell string. -----------

HELPER_REQ = os.environ.get("TSX_SETUP_HELPER_REQ", os.path.join(RUN_DIR, "setup-helper"))
HELPER_RESP = os.environ.get("TSX_SETUP_HELPER_RESP", os.path.join(RUN_DIR, "setup-helper.resp"))
# tsx-config show takes seconds on a panel (it reads panel.conf once per
# key), and more while the panel is busy. apply and rootpw can restart
# services and take longer. A request that runs out of time gets an error,
# but the helper still runs it.
HELPER_TIMEOUT = float(os.environ.get("TSX_SETUP_HELPER_TIMEOUT", "30"))
HELPER_SLOW_TIMEOUT = max(HELPER_TIMEOUT, 120.0)
_helper_lock = threading.Lock()


def _helper_drain(path):
    """Best-effort: discard any bytes already sitting in RESP before sending
    a new request, in case an earlier transaction's reader gave up early
    (tsx-setup-helper's own writes never block -- see its comments -- so a
    stale reply can otherwise sit there for the next, unrelated request).
    A stale reply that arrives after the drain is skipped by its tag (see
    helper_call)."""
    try:
        fd = os.open(path, os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NONBLOCK)
    except OSError:
        return
    try:
        while True:
            try:
                if not os.read(fd, 4096):
                    break
            except (BlockingIOError, OSError):
                break
    finally:
        os.close(fd)


def helper_call(line, timeout=None):
    """One request, one reply. Returns (ok, text). Never raises: any failure
    (helper not running, timeout, closed channel) comes back as (False,
    message) so a caller can turn it into a normal HTTP error instead of a
    crash.

    Each request has a new random tag ("#TAG CMD REST"), and tsx-setup-helper
    puts the tag in front of its reply. This reads only the reply with the
    tag of this request. A reply with another tag is late: the reply to a
    request that timed out, or to a tsx-setupd that stopped before it read
    the reply. It is skipped, so it never counts as the reply to this
    request."""
    tag = "#" + secrets.token_hex(8)
    want = (tag + " ").encode()
    with _helper_lock:
        _helper_drain(HELPER_RESP)
        try:
            wfd = os.open(HELPER_REQ, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_NONBLOCK)
        except OSError as e:
            return False, "tsx-setup-helper not listening: %s" % e
        try:
            os.write(wfd, (tag + " " + line + "\n").encode())
        except OSError as e:
            os.close(wfd)
            return False, "tsx-setup-helper: write failed: %s" % e
        os.close(wfd)
        try:
            rfd = os.open(HELPER_RESP, os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NONBLOCK)
        except OSError as e:
            return False, "tsx-setup-helper response channel: %s" % e
        try:
            buf = b""
            deadline = time.monotonic() + (HELPER_TIMEOUT if timeout is None else timeout)
            while True:
                lines = buf.split(b"\n")
                buf = lines.pop()    # the start of a line that is not complete yet
                reply = next((one[len(want):] for one in lines if one.startswith(want)), None)
                if reply is not None:
                    break
                remaining = deadline - time.monotonic()
                if remaining <= 0:
                    return False, "tsx-setup-helper did not answer in time"
                r, _w, _x = select.select([rfd], [], [], remaining)
                if not r:
                    continue
                chunk = os.read(rfd, 4096)
                if not chunk:
                    return False, "tsx-setup-helper closed the response channel"
                buf += chunk
        finally:
            os.close(rfd)
    resp = reply.decode(errors="replace")
    if resp.startswith("ok"):
        return True, resp[2:].lstrip()
    if resp.startswith("err "):
        return False, resp[4:]
    return False, "unexpected reply from tsx-setup-helper"


def tcfg_show():
    ok, text = helper_call("show")
    if not ok:
        sys.stderr.write("tsx-setupd: tsx-config show via helper failed: %s\n" % text)
        return {}
    fields = {}
    for line in text.replace("\x01", "\n").splitlines():
        if "=" in line:
            k, v = line.split("=", 1)
            fields[k] = v
    return fields


def tcfg_rev():
    """The revision of panel.conf (tsx-setup-helper "rev"), or None."""
    ok, text = helper_call("rev")
    if not ok or not text:
        sys.stderr.write("tsx-setupd: the revision of panel.conf via helper failed: %s\n" % text)
        return None
    return text


def tcfg_show_rev():
    """The values of panel.conf and the revision that belongs to them. The
    revision is read first. If the file changes during the read, the page
    gets the older revision, and a save from that page is refused. So a save
    never goes through with values that the page did not show. A second
    read of the revision tells whether a new try can get a pair that matches."""
    rev = shown = None
    for _ in range(3):
        rev = tcfg_rev()
        shown = tcfg_show()
        if rev is not None and tcfg_rev() == rev:
            break
    return shown, rev


def tcfg_set(key, val):
    if "\n" in val or "\r" in val:
        return False, "value cannot contain a newline"
    return helper_call("set %s %s" % (key, val))


def tcfg_unset(key):
    helper_call("unset %s" % key)


def tcfg_apply():
    return helper_call("apply", HELPER_SLOW_TIMEOUT)


# ServerManager.watch_forever polls is_configured() forever (every POLL_S),
# and lan_allowed() calls it once per request. A short cache keeps an idle,
# already-configured panel from forking tsx-config and reading panel.conf
# over the helper every single second for the rest of its life. A save
# (handle_submit) calls restart_kiosk() and clear_setup_open() directly
# anyway. So the cache only delays the close of the LAN listener of a
# freshly-configured panel by a few seconds. It does not delay the load of HA.
_CONFIGURED_CACHE_S = 4.0
_configured_cache = {"at": float("-inf"), "val": False, "rev": None, "ha": None}
_configured_cache_lock = threading.Lock()


def has_url(shown):
    """True if the kiosk has a URL: KIOSK_URL of panel.conf (shown is the
    output of tcfg_show), else the image default that the kiosk falls back
    to (kiosk-session: /etc/kiosk.conf, then the panel.conf override). An
    install with --kiosk-url and no panel.conf sets the URL only there."""
    v = shown.get("KIOSK_URL")
    if not (v and v.strip()):
        v = load_conf(KIOSK_CONF_PATH).get("KIOSK_URL")
    return bool(v and v.strip())


def ha_url_from(shown):
    """The URL of the Home Assistant of this panel, for the login: HA_URL of
    panel.conf, else the origin of the page URL of the kiosk (KIOSK_URL of
    panel.conf, else of /etc/kiosk.conf). None when the panel has neither.
    Only the origin counts ("https://ha.example.org:8123")."""
    for v in (shown.get("HA_URL"), shown.get("KIOSK_URL"),
              load_conf(KIOSK_CONF_PATH).get("KIOSK_URL") if kiosk_enabled() else None):
        url = setup_ha_login.normalize_ha_url(v) if v and v.strip() else None
        if url:
            return url
    return None


def is_configured_from(shown):
    """Is the panel configured? shown is the output of tcfg_show. With a
    kiosk, it needs a page URL. With no kiosk, panel.conf needs at least one
    key (a line of tcfg_show that is not a comment of tsx-config)."""
    if kiosk_enabled():
        return has_url(shown)
    return any(re.fullmatch(r"[A-Z][A-Z0-9_]*", k) for k in shown)


def _refresh_configured():
    """Read panel.conf again when its revision changed (at most every
    _CONFIGURED_CACHE_S): is the panel configured, and the Home Assistant URL."""
    # tsx-config show takes seconds on a slow panel. So the check reads the
    # values again only when the revision of panel.conf (one sha256 in the
    # helper) has changed since the last read.
    with _configured_cache_lock:
        now = time.monotonic()
        if now - _configured_cache["at"] > _CONFIGURED_CACHE_S:
            rev = tcfg_rev()
            if rev is None or rev != _configured_cache.get("rev"):
                shown = tcfg_show()
                _configured_cache["val"] = is_configured_from(shown)
                _configured_cache["ha"] = ha_url_from(shown)
                _configured_cache["rev"] = rev
            _configured_cache["at"] = now
        return _configured_cache["val"], _configured_cache["ha"]


def is_configured():
    return _refresh_configured()[0]


def ha_url():
    """The Home Assistant URL for the login (ha_url_from), or None."""
    return _refresh_configured()[1]


def _invalidate_configured_cache():
    """Call this right after a successful save. Otherwise is_configured()
    might not notice for up to _CONFIGURED_CACHE_S, and the response of the
    state API to that same save would look stale."""
    with _configured_cache_lock:
        _configured_cache["at"] = float("-inf")
        _configured_cache["rev"] = None


def _run_flag_path():
    return os.path.join(RUN_DIR, "setup-open")


def _uptime_seconds():
    """Monotonic (seconds since boot), never wall-clock: the panel has no
    RTC, so it boots with a stale clock that NTP steps by months once
    chrony syncs; a wall-clock comparison spanning that step would read a
    just-opened window as instantly expired or good for months. /run is
    tmpfs, so a reboot always starts both the flag and this clock fresh
    together. Matches tsx-kiosk-url and tsx-config setup, which read the
    same file the same way in shell (awk over /proc/uptime)."""
    try:
        with open("/proc/uptime") as f:
            return int(float(f.read().split()[0]))
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
        return 0


def setup_open_fresh():
    try:
        with open(_run_flag_path(), "r") as f:
            opened_at = int(f.read().strip())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return False
    now = _uptime_seconds()
    return 0 <= now - opened_at <= WINDOW


def clear_setup_open():
    helper_call("clear-setup-open")


def pairing_open():
    """The pairing code works: the LAN is allowed, and the panel is not
    configured or the setup window is open."""
    return LAN_ON and (not is_configured() or setup_open_fresh())


def login_available():
    """"Log in with Home Assistant" works: the LAN is allowed, the login is on
    (TSX_SETUP_HA_LOGIN) and the panel knows the URL of its Home Assistant."""
    return LAN_ON and HA_LOGIN and ha_url() is not None


def lan_allowed():
    """The LAN can reach the page: with the pairing code or with the login."""
    return pairing_open() or login_available()


def restart_kiosk():
    helper_call("kiosk-restart", HELPER_SLOW_TIMEOUT)


def set_root_password(pw):
    if "\n" in pw or "\r" in pw or len(pw) < 8:
        return False
    ok, _text = helper_call("rootpw %s" % pw, HELPER_SLOW_TIMEOUT)
    return ok


# ---- time zone list ---------------------------------------------------

_TZ_FALLBACK = ["UTC", "America/New_York", "America/Chicago", "America/Denver",
                "America/Los_Angeles", "America/Anchorage", "Europe/London",
                "Europe/Berlin", "Europe/Madrid", "Asia/Tokyo", "Asia/Shanghai",
                "Asia/Kolkata", "Australia/Sydney", "Pacific/Auckland"]
_TZ_SKIP_DIRS = {"posix", "right", "SystemV"}
_TZ_SKIP_FILES = {"posixrules", "leapseconds", "tzdata.zi", "iso3166.tab",
                   "zone.tab", "zone1970.tab", "leap-seconds.list", "Factory"}


def zone_list():
    try:
        zones = []
        for root, dirs, files in os.walk(ZONEINFO_DIR):
            dirs[:] = [d for d in dirs if d not in _TZ_SKIP_DIRS and not d.startswith(".")]
            rel = os.path.relpath(root, ZONEINFO_DIR)
            for fn in files:
                if fn in _TZ_SKIP_FILES or fn.endswith(".tab") or "." in fn:
                    continue
                zones.append(fn if rel == "." else "%s/%s" % (rel, fn))
        zones = sorted(set(zones))
        if zones:
            return zones
    except OSError:
        pass
    return _TZ_FALLBACK


# ---- LAN pairing (loopback never needs any of this) --------------------

class Pairing:
    def __init__(self):
        self.lock = threading.Lock()
        self.code = None
        self.code_at = 0.0
        self.attempts = {}   # ip -> [count, first_attempt_at]

    def current_code(self):
        with self.lock:
            now = time.monotonic()
            if self.code is None or now - self.code_at > WINDOW:
                self.code = "%06d" % secrets.randbelow(1000000)
                self.code_at = now
            return self.code, self.code_at

    def allow_attempt(self, ip):
        with self.lock:
            now = time.monotonic()
            rec = self.attempts.get(ip)
            if rec is None or now - rec[1] > WINDOW:
                self.attempts[ip] = [1, now]
                return True
            rec[0] += 1
            rec[1] = now
            return rec[0] <= 10   # 10 tries per window per source address

    def check(self, code):
        """True for the current code while it is valid."""
        with self.lock:
            now = time.monotonic()
            if self.code is None or now - self.code_at > WINDOW:
                return False
            return bool(code) and hmac.compare_digest(str(code), self.code)


# ---- sessions: the server-side list (a logout or a revoke ends one at once) ----

class Sessions:
    """The sessions of the LAN clients. kind "ha": a login with Home
    Assistant (an administrator), valid SESSION_S seconds after the login and
    IDLE_S seconds after the last request. kind "pair": the pairing code,
    valid for WINDOW seconds and only while pairing_open(). Each session has
    a public id (for the list and a revoke) and its own CSRF token."""
    MAX = 32

    def __init__(self):
        self.lock = threading.Lock()
        self.items = {}   # token -> record

    def new(self, kind, ip, user="", user_id=""):
        now = time.monotonic()
        token = secrets.token_urlsafe(32)
        rec = {"id": secrets.token_hex(8), "kind": kind, "user": user, "user_id": user_id, "ip": ip,
               "created": now, "last": now, "expires": now + (SESSION_S if kind == "ha" else WINDOW),
               "csrf": secrets.token_urlsafe(24)}
        with self.lock:
            self._prune(now)
            while len(self.items) >= self.MAX:
                oldest = min(self.items, key=lambda t: self.items[t]["last"])
                del self.items[oldest]
            self.items[token] = rec
        return token, rec

    def _prune(self, now):
        for t in [t for t, r in self.items.items() if not self._alive(r, now)]:
            del self.items[t]

    @staticmethod
    def _alive(rec, now):
        if now > rec["expires"]:
            return False
        return rec["kind"] != "ha" or now - rec["last"] <= IDLE_S

    def get(self, token, touch=True):
        """The record of a valid session, or None."""
        if not token:
            return None
        with self.lock:
            rec = self.items.get(token)
            now = time.monotonic()
            if rec is None:
                return None
            if not self._alive(rec, now):
                del self.items[token]
                return None
            if touch:
                rec["last"] = now
            return dict(rec)

    def drop(self, token):
        with self.lock:
            return self.items.pop(token, None) is not None

    def drop_id(self, sid):
        with self.lock:
            for t, r in list(self.items.items()):
                if hmac.compare_digest(r["id"], str(sid)):
                    del self.items[t]
                    return True
        return False

    def drop_all(self):
        with self.lock:
            n = len(self.items)
            self.items.clear()
            return n

    def listing(self, current_token=None):
        now = time.monotonic()
        with self.lock:
            self._prune(now)
            return [{"id": r["id"], "kind": r["kind"], "user": r["user"], "ip": r["ip"],
                     "age": int(now - r["created"]), "idle": int(now - r["last"]),
                     "expires_in": int(r["expires"] - now), "current": t == current_token}
                    for t, r in sorted(self.items.items(), key=lambda it: it[1]["created"])]


class RateLimit:
    """At most `limit` events per `window` seconds for one key."""

    def __init__(self, limit, window):
        self.limit, self.window = limit, window
        self.lock = threading.Lock()
        self.events = {}

    def allow(self, key):
        with self.lock:
            now = time.monotonic()
            if len(self.events) > 1024:
                self.events = {k: v for k, v in self.events.items() if v and now - v[-1] < self.window}
            ev = [t for t in self.events.get(key, []) if now - t < self.window]
            if len(ev) >= self.limit:
                self.events[key] = ev
                return False
            ev.append(now)
            self.events[key] = ev
            return True


SESSIONS = Sessions()
# The login: the starts per address and for all, the returns from Home
# Assistant per address.
LOGIN_START_IP = RateLimit(20, 600)
LOGIN_START_ALL = RateLimit(60, 600)
LOGIN_RETURN_IP = RateLimit(30, 600)
# The logins that wait for the return from Home Assistant: state -> record
PENDING = {}
PENDING_LOCK = threading.Lock()
PENDING_MAX = 64
PENDING_S = 600
# The CSRF token of the loopback clients (the kiosk browser on the panel)
LOOPBACK_CSRF = secrets.token_urlsafe(24)
PAIRING = Pairing()

# Fields that tsx-config itself owns. The page sends a field only when the
# user changed it. Blank means "leave as it is", unless the key is in
# CLEARABLE_BLANK. There, the own validation of tsx-config already treats an
# empty value as meaningful ("off" or "allow any"). KIOSK_URL is handled
# separately below (required). These are the keys of the base system. A
# plugin adds its own keys (see "plugins" below).
BASE_SIMPLE_KEYS = ["PANEL_NAME", "TZ_NAME", "SSH_AUTHORIZED_KEY",
                    "KERNEL_FLAVOR", "BLANK_TIMEOUT", "ORIENTATION", "KIOSK_DARK",
                    "PRESENCE_WAKE", "PRESENCE_DISTANCE_MM", "PRESENCE_HOLD_S",
                    "AUTO_BRIGHTNESS", "ALS_SCALE"]
# Empty = cleared. The page pre-fills these keys, so an empty one was cleared
# on purpose, and the server removes the key.
BASE_CLEARABLE_BLANK = {"PRESENCE_WAKE", "PRESENCE_DISTANCE_MM",
                        "PRESENCE_HOLD_S", "AUTO_BRIGHTNESS", "ALS_SCALE"}
# the settings of the base that a panel can lack: name -> the keys that a save
# leaves as they are
BASE_UNAVAILABLE_DROPS = {"AUTO_BRIGHTNESS": ("AUTO_BRIGHTNESS", "ALS_SCALE"),
                          "PRESENCE_WAKE": ("PRESENCE_WAKE", "PRESENCE_DISTANCE_MM", "PRESENCE_HOLD_S")}
BASE_STATE_KEYS = ["KIOSK_URL"] + BASE_SIMPLE_KEYS

# ---- plugins -------------------------------------------------------------------
# A package that adds fields to the page ships one Python file in PLUGIN_DIR.
# The file says which keys it owns, gives the HTML and the script of its
# fields, and checks its own values. The contract is in the docstring of
# rootfs/overlay/usr/local/share/tsx/setup.d/ha.py. A plugin that fails to load
# is skipped, and the page works without it.


class _Ctx:
    """What a plugin may use of this program."""
    no_zeroconf = NO_ZEROCONF

    @staticmethod
    def validate(key, val):
        return tcfg_validate(key, val)

    @staticmethod
    def show():
        return tcfg_show()

    @staticmethod
    def helper_call(line, timeout=None):
        """One request to tsx-setup-helper: (ok, text). See helper_call."""
        return helper_call(line, timeout)

    @staticmethod
    def lan_allowed():
        """The LAN can reach the page (the pairing window or the login)."""
        return lan_allowed()

    @staticmethod
    def pairing_open():
        return pairing_open()

    @staticmethod
    def login_available():
        return login_available()


def load_plugins(path=None):
    plugins = []
    path = path or PLUGIN_DIR
    try:
        names = sorted(n for n in os.listdir(path) if n.endswith(".py") and not n.startswith("_"))
    except OSError:
        return plugins
    for n in names:
        try:
            spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("tsx_setup_plugin_" + n[:-3], os.path.join(path, n))
            mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
            spec.loader.exec_module(mod)
            if hasattr(mod, "init"):
                mod.init(_Ctx)
        except Exception as e:  # a broken plugin must not stop the page
            sys.stderr.write("tsx-setupd: plugin %s skipped: %s\n" % (n, e))
            continue
        plugins.append(mod)
    return plugins


PLUGINS = load_plugins()
SIMPLE_KEYS = BASE_SIMPLE_KEYS + [k for p in PLUGINS for k in getattr(p, "SIMPLE_KEYS", [])]
CLEARABLE_BLANK = set(BASE_CLEARABLE_BLANK).union(*[set(getattr(p, "CLEARABLE_BLANK", ())) for p in PLUGINS])
STATE_KEYS = set(BASE_STATE_KEYS).union(*[set(getattr(p, "STATE_KEYS", getattr(p, "SIMPLE_KEYS", ()))) for p in PLUGINS])
UNAVAILABLE_DROPS = dict(BASE_UNAVAILABLE_DROPS)
for _p in PLUGINS:
    UNAVAILABLE_DROPS.update(getattr(_p, "UNAVAILABLE_DROPS", {}))
# the keys that only a service of tsx-kiosk reads (used when the mode has no kiosk)
KIOSK_ONLY_KEYS = set(BASE_KIOSK_ONLY_KEYS).union(*[set(getattr(p, "KIOSK_ONLY_KEYS", ())) for p in PLUGINS])

_TEXT_DEFAULTS = {
    "url_label": "Page URL",
    "url_placeholder": "https://example.org",
    "url_required": "the page URL is required",
    "submit_label": "Save",
    "done_text": "Saved. This panel will load the page now.",
}
# With no kiosk, the panel loads no page. These words replace the words of a
# plugin for the save button and for the text after a save.
_TEXT_NO_KIOSK = {
    "submit_label": "Save",
    "done_text": "Saved. The settings are in use now.",
}


def page_text(name):
    if not kiosk_enabled() and name in _TEXT_NO_KIOSK:
        return _TEXT_NO_KIOSK[name]
    for p in PLUGINS:
        v = getattr(p, "TEXT", {}).get(name)
        if v:
            return v
    return _TEXT_DEFAULTS[name]


def hw_unavailable():
    """The settings that this panel cannot use, with the reason
    (hw.conf of tsx-hw. A missing file = a panel with all parts)."""
    hw = load_conf(HW_CONF_PATH)
    out = {}
    # ALS=no or LIGHT=no: a board names its light sensor part either way
    if hw.get("ALS") == "no" or hw.get("LIGHT") == "no":
        out["AUTO_BRIGHTNESS"] = "no ambient light sensor on this panel"
    if hw.get("PRESENCE") == "no":
        out["PRESENCE_WAKE"] = "no distance sensor on this panel"
    for p in PLUGINS:
        if hasattr(p, "unavailable"):
            out.update(p.unavailable(hw))
    return out


STALE_MESSAGE = ("The settings of the panel changed after this page loaded. Nothing is saved. "
                 "Reload the page, then make your changes again.")


# The keys that the kiosk reads only at its start: a save that changes one
# restarts the kiosk, also from a remote session.
KIOSK_START_KEYS = {"KIOSK_URL", "KIOSK_DARK", "HA_LOGIN_METHOD", "HA_TOKEN"}


def handle_submit(fields, revision=None, on_screen=True):
    """Save the fields that the page sent: only the fields that the user
    changed. revision is the revision of panel.conf that the page loaded.
    on_screen: the setup page is on the screen of the panel (a loopback
    request, a pairing session or the open window). Then the save closes the
    window and restarts the kiosk, as before. A save of a remote session
    (the login with Home Assistant) restarts the kiosk only when a key of
    KIOSK_START_KEYS changed. Returns (HTTP status, JSON object).

    If the page sends a field and panel.conf changed after that revision,
    nothing is written (409). A value that is already stored is not written
    again. tsx-config apply runs only when a key or the root password changed."""
    errors = {}
    # A setting that this panel cannot use is not sent by the page. Keep what
    # panel.conf has (tsx-config apply treats VOICE=on as off).
    gone = set()
    for name in hw_unavailable():
        gone.add(name)
        gone.update(UNAVAILABLE_DROPS.get(name, ()))
    kiosk = kiosk_enabled()
    if not kiosk:
        gone.update(KIOSK_ONLY_KEYS)    # no kiosk reads them: a save leaves them as they are
    fields = {k: v for k, v in fields.items() if k not in gone}

    # The revision first, then the values (see tcfg_show_rev). A change of
    # the file after this read and before the writes below is not seen: no
    # writer of panel.conf takes a lock.
    current = tcfg_rev()
    stored = tcfg_show()
    if fields:
        if current is None:
            return 502, {"errors": {"_revision": "could not read the settings of the panel. Try again."}}
        if not isinstance(revision, str) or revision != current:
            return 409, {"errors": {"_revision": STALE_MESSAGE}, "stale": True}

    to_set = {}
    to_unset = []
    post_unset = []

    if not kiosk:
        pass    # no page URL: the panel loads no page
    elif "KIOSK_URL" in fields:
        url = fields["KIOSK_URL"]
        if not isinstance(url, str) or not url.strip():
            errors["KIOSK_URL"] = page_text("url_required")
        elif not tcfg_validate("KIOSK_URL", url.strip()):
            errors["KIOSK_URL"] = "must be a valid http(s) URL with no spaces"
        else:
            to_set["KIOSK_URL"] = url.strip()
    elif not has_url(stored):
        # the first save: the page sends the URL, because the field was empty
        errors["KIOSK_URL"] = page_text("url_required")

    for p in PLUGINS:
        if hasattr(p, "submit"):
            p_set, p_unset, p_err, p_post = p.submit(fields)
            to_set.update(p_set)
            to_unset.extend(p_unset)
            errors.update(p_err)
            post_unset.extend(p_post)

    for key in SIMPLE_KEYS:
        if key not in fields:
            continue
        val = fields[key]
        if not isinstance(val, str):
            errors[key] = "invalid value"
            continue
        if val == "":
            if key in CLEARABLE_BLANK:
                # Validate it like any value, before anything is written. A
                # set that fails half-way leaves KIOSK_URL written (so the
                # LAN listener closes) with nothing applied.
                if tcfg_validate(key, val):
                    to_unset.append(key)    # cleared: remove the key, never KEY=""
                else:
                    errors[key] = "invalid value"
            continue
        if not tcfg_validate(key, val):
            errors[key] = "invalid value"
            continue
        to_set[key] = val

    rootpw = fields.get("ROOT_PASSWORD") or ""
    if not isinstance(rootpw, str):
        errors["ROOT_PASSWORD"] = "invalid value"
    elif rootpw and ("\n" in rootpw or "\r" in rootpw):
        errors["ROOT_PASSWORD"] = "cannot contain a newline"
    elif rootpw and len(rootpw) < 8:
        errors["ROOT_PASSWORD"] = "must be at least 8 characters"

    if errors:
        return 400, {"errors": errors}

    # Leave out what panel.conf already has. show masks a secret, so a
    # secret that the page sent is always written.
    masked = {k for k, v in stored.items() if v == "********"}
    writes = {k: v for k, v in to_set.items() if k in masked or stored.get(k) != v}
    removes = [k for k in dict.fromkeys(to_unset + post_unset) if k in stored and k not in to_set]
    changed = sorted(set(writes) | set(removes) | ({"ROOT_PASSWORD"} if rootpw else set()))

    if changed:
        for key, val in writes.items():
            ok, err = tcfg_set(key, val)
            if not ok:
                errors[key] = err or "failed to save"
        for key in removes:
            tcfg_unset(key)
        if errors:
            return 400, {"errors": errors}

        if rootpw:
            if not set_root_password(rootpw):
                return 500, {"errors": {"ROOT_PASSWORD": "failed to change the root password"}}

        ok, err = tcfg_apply()
        if not ok:
            return 500, {"errors": {"_apply": err or "tsx-config apply failed"}}

    # Also a save with no change closes the window: the user is done, and
    # the kiosk loads the page again.
    _invalidate_configured_cache()
    restarted = False
    if on_screen:
        clear_setup_open()
    if kiosk and (on_screen or KIOSK_START_KEYS & set(changed)):
        restart_kiosk()
        restarted = True
    return 200, {"ok": True, "changed": changed, "applied": bool(changed), "kiosk_restarted": restarted}


# ---- the Panel section: live controls (tsx-panelctl and tsx-config) -------

def _int(v, low, high):
    """An int (not a bool) from low to high, else None."""
    if isinstance(v, bool) or not isinstance(v, (int, float)) or v != int(v):
        return None
    v = int(v)
    return v if low <= v <= high else None


def _first_int(text):
    try:
        return int(str(text).split()[0])
    except (ValueError, IndexError):
        return None


_COLOR_RE = re.compile(r"#[0-9a-fA-F]{6}")


def _hex_color(r, g, b):
    return "#%02x%02x%02x" % (max(0, min(255, r)), max(0, min(255, g)), max(0, min(255, b)))


def panel_state():
    """The controls that this panel has, with their values ({"owner",
    "controls"}), from `tsx-panelctl state` (helper "panel-state"). Returns
    (object, None) or (None, error text)."""
    ok, text = helper_call("panel-state", HELPER_TIMEOUT)
    if not ok:
        return None, text or "could not read the panel"
    vals = {}
    for line in text.replace("\x01", "\n").splitlines():
        k, _, v = line.partition(" ")
        if k:
            vals[k] = v.strip()
    c = {}
    if vals.get("screen") in ("on", "off"):
        c["screen"] = {"on": vals["screen"] == "on"}
    n = _first_int(vals.get("backlight-pct"))
    if n is not None and 1 <= n <= 100:
        c["backlight"] = {"value": n}
    n = _first_int(vals.get("blank-timeout"))
    if n is not None:
        c["blank_timeout"] = {"value": n}
    n = _first_int(vals.get("dim-timeout"))
    if n is not None:
        c["dim_timeout"] = {"value": n}
    n = _first_int(vals.get("dim-level"))
    if n is not None:
        c["dim_level"] = {"value": n}
    if vals.get("update-mode") in ("auto", "manual"):
        c["update_mode"] = {"value": vals["update-mode"]}
    if vals.get("auto-restart") in ("on", "off"):
        c["auto_restart"] = {"on": vals["auto-restart"] == "on"}
    n = _first_int(vals.get("volume"))
    if n is not None:
        c["volume"] = {"value": n}
        # Only the Sendspin player has a mute (tsx-panelctl, "Volume").
        if vals.get("mute") in ("on", "off"):
            c["volume"]["muted"] = vals["mute"] == "on"
    if "ledbar" in vals:
        rgb = [_first_int(x) or 0 for x in (vals["ledbar"].split() + ["0", "0", "0"])[:3]]
        top = max(rgb)
        c["ledbar"] = {"on": top > 0, "brightness": max(1, top),
                       "color": _hex_color(*[round(x * 255 / top) if top else 255 for x in rgb])}
    if "keypad-led" in vals:
        n = _first_int(vals["keypad-led"]) or 0
        c["key_leds"] = {"on": n > 0, "level": max(1, min(255, n))}
    if "lights" in vals:
        w = vals["lights"].split()
        if len(w) == 5 and w[0] in ("on", "off"):
            nums = [_first_int(x) or 0 for x in w[1:]]
            c["lights"] = {"on": w[0] == "on", "brightness": max(1, min(100, nums[0])), "color": _hex_color(*nums[1:])}
    return {"owner": vals.get("owner", "none"), "controls": c}, None


def _panel_send(words):
    ok, text = helper_call("panel " + words, HELPER_TIMEOUT)
    return (200, {"ok": True}) if ok else (502, {"error": text or "the panel did not take the command"})


def _panel_setting(key, val):
    """A time of the screen: panel.conf through tsx-config (set and apply),
    the same keys that the Home Assistant numbers write. The answer has the
    revision of panel.conf before and after, so that the page can keep its
    form."""
    if not tcfg_validate(key, str(val)):
        return 400, {"error": "this panel does not take %s=%s" % (key, val)}
    before = tcfg_rev()
    ok, err = tcfg_set(key, str(val))
    if not ok:
        return 400, {"error": err or "could not save %s" % key}
    ok, err = tcfg_apply()
    if not ok:
        return 500, {"error": err or "tsx-config apply failed"}
    _invalidate_configured_cache()
    return 200, {"ok": True, "revision_before": before, "revision": tcfg_rev()}


def panel_set(control, value):
    """One change of a live control. Returns (HTTP status, JSON object)."""
    if control == "screen" and isinstance(value, bool):
        return _panel_send("blank off" if value else "blank on")
    if control == "backlight" and _int(value, 1, 100) is not None:
        return _panel_send("brightness-pct %d" % _int(value, 1, 100))
    if control == "volume" and _int(value, 0, 100) is not None:
        return _panel_send("volume %d" % _int(value, 0, 100))
    if control == "mute" and isinstance(value, bool):
        return _panel_send("mute on" if value else "mute off")
    if control == "blank_timeout" and _int(value, 0, 86400) is not None:
        return _panel_setting("BLANK_TIMEOUT", _int(value, 0, 86400))
    if control == "dim_timeout" and _int(value, 0, 86400) is not None:
        return _panel_setting("DIM_TIMEOUT", _int(value, 0, 86400))
    if control == "dim_level" and _int(value, 1, 100) is not None:
        return _panel_setting("DIM_LEVEL", _int(value, 1, 100))
    if control == "update_mode" and value in ("auto", "manual"):
        return _panel_setting("UPDATE_MODE", value)
    if control == "auto_restart" and isinstance(value, bool):
        return _panel_setting("UPDATE_AUTO_RESTART", "on" if value else "off")
    if control in ("ledbar", "lights") and isinstance(value, dict) and isinstance(value.get("on"), bool):
        if not value["on"]:
            return _panel_send("%s off" % control)
        bri = _int(value.get("brightness", 100), 1, 100)
        color = value.get("color", "#ffffff")
        if bri is None or not isinstance(color, str) or not _COLOR_RE.fullmatch(color):
            return 400, {"error": "the light needs a brightness from 1 to 100 and a color #rrggbb"}
        r, g, b = (int(color[i:i + 2], 16) for i in (1, 3, 5))
        if control == "lights":
            return _panel_send("lights set %d %d %d %d" % (bri, r, g, b))
        # the LED bar takes R G B from 0 to 100: the color at the brightness
        return _panel_send("ledbar set %d %d %d" % tuple(round(x * bri / 255) for x in (r, g, b)))
    if control == "key_leds" and isinstance(value, dict) and isinstance(value.get("on"), bool):
        if not value["on"]:
            return _panel_send("keypad led off")
        level = _int(value.get("level", 255), 1, 255)
        if level is None:
            return 400, {"error": "the key LEDs need a level from 1 to 255"}
        return _panel_send("keypad led %d" % level)
    return 400, {"error": "unknown control or a value out of range"}


# ---- "Log in with Home Assistant" -----------------------------------------

_HOST_RE = re.compile(r"(?:[A-Za-z0-9](?:[A-Za-z0-9.-]{0,251}[A-Za-z0-9])?|\[[0-9A-Fa-f:.]+\])(?::[0-9]{1,5})?")


def _pending_put(state, rec):
    with PENDING_LOCK:
        now = time.monotonic()
        for k in [k for k, r in PENDING.items() if now - r["created"] > PENDING_S]:
            del PENDING[k]
        while len(PENDING) >= PENDING_MAX:
            del PENDING[min(PENDING, key=lambda k: PENDING[k]["created"])]
        PENDING[state] = rec


def _pending_take(state):
    with PENDING_LOCK:
        rec = PENDING.pop(state, None) if isinstance(state, str) else None
    if rec is None or time.monotonic() - rec["created"] > PENDING_S:
        return None
    return rec


def ha_login_finish(rec, code):
    """The steps on the panel after the return from Home Assistant: the code
    for tokens, the user, the revoke. Returns (user dict, None) or (None,
    error text). No token stays in this process."""
    ha = rec["ha"]
    try:
        tokens = setup_ha_login.exchange_code(ha, code, rec["client_id"])
    except setup_ha_login.HaError as e:
        return None, str(e)
    try:
        user = setup_ha_login.current_user(ha, tokens["access_token"])
    except setup_ha_login.HaError as e:
        user, err = None, str(e)
    else:
        err = None
    finally:
        revoked = setup_ha_login.revoke(ha, tokens.get("refresh_token"))
        if tokens.get("refresh_token") and not revoked:
            sys.stderr.write("tsx-setupd: login: Home Assistant did not take the revoke of the refresh token\n")
        tokens.clear()
    return user, err


LOGIN_PAGE = """<!doctype html>
<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
@@REFRESH@@<title>Panel setup</title>
<style>
  :root { --bg:#0b0e12; --fg:#e8ecf1; --muted:#8b96a3; --accent:#4fb2e8; }
  body { background:var(--bg); color:var(--fg); font-family:system-ui,sans-serif;
         display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:center; min-height:100vh; margin:0; }
  .box { max-width:640px; padding:32px; text-align:center; }
  h1 { font-size:1.4rem; }
  p { color:var(--muted); line-height:1.5; }
  a { color:var(--accent); }
</style></head>
<body><div class="box">
  <h1>@@TITLE@@</h1>
  <p>@@TEXT@@</p>
  <p><a href="/setup">Open the setup page</a></p>
</div>@@SCRIPT@@</body></html>
"""


def login_page(title, text, go=False):
    """A small page for the steps of the login. go=True opens the setup page
    at once: a navigation that starts on this page sends the session cookie
    (SameSite=Strict), unlike the redirect chain that started at Home
    Assistant."""
    esc = lambda t: t.replace("&", "&amp;").replace("<", "&lt;").replace(">", "&gt;")
    out = LOGIN_PAGE.replace("@@TITLE@@", esc(title)).replace("@@TEXT@@", esc(text))
    out = out.replace("@@REFRESH@@", '<meta http-equiv="refresh" content="0;url=/setup">\n' if go else "")
    return out.replace("@@SCRIPT@@", '<script>location.replace("/setup");</script>' if go else "")


# ---- HTTP -----------------------------------------------------------------

def _cookie(name, value, path="/setup", max_age=None, http_only=True, same_site="Strict"):
    parts = ["%s=%s" % (name, value), "Path=%s" % path, "SameSite=%s" % same_site]
    if http_only:
        parts.append("HttpOnly")
    if max_age is not None:
        parts.append("Max-Age=%d" % max_age)
    return "; ".join(parts)


class Handler(BaseHTTPRequestHandler):
    server_version = "tsx-setupd"
    sys_version = ""

    def _loopback(self):
        ip = self.client_address[0]
        return ip in ("127.0.0.1", "::1") or ip.startswith("127.")

    def _cookie_value(self, name):
        cookie = self.headers.get("Cookie", "") or ""
        for part in cookie.split(";"):
            part = part.strip()
            if part.startswith(name + "="):
                return part[len(name) + 1:]
        return None

    def _session_token(self):
        return self._cookie_value("tsxsetup")

    def _session(self):
        """The session record of a LAN client, or None. A pairing session
        counts only while the pairing window is open."""
        if not hasattr(self, "_sess_cache"):
            rec = SESSIONS.get(self._session_token())
            if rec is not None and rec["kind"] == "pair" and not pairing_open():
                rec = None
            if rec is not None and rec["kind"] == "ha" and not login_available():
                rec = None
            self._sess_cache = rec
        return self._sess_cache

    def _authorized(self):
        if self._loopback():
            return True
        if not lan_allowed():
            return False
        return self._session() is not None

    def _on_screen(self):
        """The request comes from the setup page on the screen, or from a
        pairing session: a save closes the window and restarts the kiosk."""
        s = None if self._loopback() else self._session()
        return self._loopback() or (s is not None and s["kind"] == "pair") or setup_open_fresh()

    def _csrf_token(self):
        """The CSRF token of this client (None without a session)."""
        if self._loopback():
            return LOOPBACK_CSRF
        s = self._session() if lan_allowed() else None
        return s["csrf"] if s else None

    def _extra_cookies(self):
        """The cookie tsxcsrf for an authorized client that does not have it yet."""
        tok = self._csrf_token()
        if tok and self._cookie_value("tsxcsrf") != tok:
            return [_cookie("tsxcsrf", tok, http_only=False, max_age=SESSION_S)]
        return []

    def _send(self, code, ctype, body, cookies=()):
        self.send_response(code)
        self.send_header("Content-Type", ctype)
        self.send_header("Content-Length", str(len(body)))
        self.send_header("Cache-Control", "no-store")
        self.send_header("X-Frame-Options", "DENY")
        self.send_header("X-Content-Type-Options", "nosniff")
        self.send_header("Referrer-Policy", "no-referrer")
        for c in cookies:
            self.send_header("Set-Cookie", c)
        self.end_headers()
        self.wfile.write(body)

    def _send_json(self, code, obj, set_cookie=None):
        cookies = list(set_cookie) if isinstance(set_cookie, (list, tuple)) else ([set_cookie] if set_cookie else [])
        self._send(code, "application/json", json.dumps(obj).encode(), cookies + self._extra_cookies())

    def _send_html(self, code, html, cookies=()):
        self._send(code, "text/html; charset=utf-8", html.encode(), list(cookies) + self._extra_cookies())

    def _redirect(self, location, cookies=()):
        self.send_response(302)
        self.send_header("Location", location)
        self.send_header("Content-Length", "0")
        self.send_header("Cache-Control", "no-store")
        self.send_header("Referrer-Policy", "no-referrer")
        for c in cookies:
            self.send_header("Set-Cookie", c)
        self.end_headers()

    def log_message(self, fmt, *args):
        # Never log the request body (POST payloads can carry secrets), and
        # never the query of the return from Home Assistant (the code).
        msg = fmt % args
        msg = re.sub(r"(/setup/auth/callback)\?\S*", r"\1?...", msg)
        sys.stderr.write("tsx-setupd: %s %s\n" % (self.address_string(), msg))

    def _read_json_body(self):
        try:
            length = int(self.headers.get("Content-Length", "0") or "0")
        except ValueError:
            length = 0
        if length > 65536:
            return None, 413
        raw = self.rfile.read(length) if length else b""
        if not raw:
            return {}, 200
        try:
            data = json.loads(raw)
        except ValueError:
            return None, 400
        if not isinstance(data, dict):
            return None, 400
        return data, 200

    def _post_checks(self, need_token):
        """The CSRF checks of a POST. Returns None when the request may go
        on, else (status, message)."""
        ctype = (self.headers.get("Content-Type") or "").split(";")[0].strip().lower()
        if ctype != "application/json":
            return 415, "the request must be JSON (Content-Type application/json)"
        origin = self.headers.get("Origin")
        host = self.headers.get("Host") or ""
        if origin is not None and origin.lower() != ("http://" + host).lower():
            return 403, "the request comes from another site"
        site = self.headers.get("Sec-Fetch-Site")
        if site is not None and site not in ("same-origin", "none"):
            return 403, "the request comes from another site"
        if need_token:
            want = self._csrf_token()
            got = self.headers.get("X-TSX-CSRF") or ""
            if not want or not hmac.compare_digest(got, want):
                return 403, "the CSRF token is missing or wrong. Reload the page."
        return None

    # ---- routes ----
    def do_GET(self):
        self.__dict__.pop("_sess_cache", None)   # one session lookup per request
        parts = urllib.parse.urlsplit(self.path)
        path = parts.path
        if path in ("/setup", "/setup/"):
            if not self._loopback() and not lan_allowed():
                self._send_html(403, PAGE_UNAVAILABLE)
                return
            self._send_html(200, PAGE if kiosk_enabled() else PAGE_NO_KIOSK)
            return
        if path == "/setup/login":
            self._login_start()
            return
        if path == "/setup/auth/callback":
            self._login_callback(urllib.parse.parse_qs(parts.query))
            return
        if path == "/setup/api/state":
            self._api_state()
            return
        if path == "/setup/api/status":
            self._api_status()
            return
        if path == "/setup/api/panel":
            self._api_panel_get()
            return
        if path == "/setup/api/sessions":
            self._api_sessions()
            return
        for p in PLUGINS:
            fn = getattr(p, "GET_ROUTES", {}).get(path)
            if fn:
                fn(self)
                return
        self.send_error(404)

    def do_POST(self):
        self.__dict__.pop("_sess_cache", None)
        path = urllib.parse.urlsplit(self.path).path
        routes = {}
        for p in PLUGINS:
            routes.update(getattr(p, "POST_ROUTES", {}))
        own = ("/setup/api/pair", "/setup/api/submit", "/setup/api/brightness-learn-reset", "/setup/api/panel",
               "/setup/api/logout", "/setup/api/sessions/revoke")
        if path not in own and path not in routes:
            self.send_error(404)
            return
        # /setup/api/pair comes before a session: it has no CSRF token
        bad = self._post_checks(need_token=path != "/setup/api/pair")
        if bad is not None:
            self._send_json(bad[0], {"error": bad[1]})
            return
        data, code = self._read_json_body()
        if data is None:
            self._send_json(code, {"error": "bad request body"})
            return
        if path == "/setup/api/pair":
            self._api_pair(data)
        elif path == "/setup/api/submit":
            self._api_submit(data)
        elif path == "/setup/api/brightness-learn-reset":
            self._api_learn_reset()
        elif path == "/setup/api/panel":
            self._api_panel_post(data)
        elif path == "/setup/api/logout":
            self._api_logout()
        elif path == "/setup/api/sessions/revoke":
            self._api_sessions_revoke(data)
        else:
            routes[path](self, data)

    def _gate_info(self):
        """What a LAN client without a session can do."""
        url = ha_url() if login_available() else None
        return {"need_pairing": True, "need_login": True, "window_seconds": WINDOW,
                "login": url is not None, "pairing": pairing_open(),
                "ha_host": urllib.parse.urlsplit(url).netloc if url else ""}

    def _api_state(self):
        loop = self._loopback()
        if not loop and not lan_allowed():
            self._send_json(403, {"error": "setup is not reachable over the network right now"})
            return
        if not self._authorized():
            self._send_json(200, self._gate_info())
            return
        shown, rev = tcfg_show_rev()
        kiosk = kiosk_enabled()
        fields = {}
        for k, v in shown.items():
            if k not in STATE_KEYS:
                continue    # a key of a package that this panel does not have
            if not kiosk and k in KIOSK_ONLY_KEYS:
                continue    # a key that no service of this panel reads
            if v == "********":
                fields[k] = None
                fields[k + "__set"] = True
            else:
                fields[k] = v
        if kiosk and "KIOSK_DARK" not in fields:
            # not in panel.conf: the page shows the value of /etc/kiosk.conf
            kd = load_conf(KIOSK_CONF_PATH).get("KIOSK_DARK")
            if kd in ("on", "off"):
                fields["KIOSK_DARK"] = kd
        sess = None if loop else self._session()
        resp = {
            "need_pairing": False,
            "configured": is_configured_from(shown),
            "kiosk": kiosk,
            "loopback": loop,
            "lan_allowed": lan_allowed(),
            "window_seconds": WINDOW,
            "revision": rev,
            "fields": fields,
            "tz_list": zone_list(),
            "unavailable": hw_unavailable(),
            "csrf": self._csrf_token(),
            "session": {"kind": "local"} if loop else {"kind": sess["kind"], "user": sess["user"]},
            "login": login_available(),
        }
        if loop:
            code, code_at = PAIRING.current_code()
            resp["pairing_code"] = code
            resp["pairing_code_remaining"] = max(0, int(WINDOW - (time.monotonic() - code_at)))
        self._send_json(200, resp)

    def _api_status(self):
        """The refresh of the page (every 20 s): the pairing code and the
        revision of panel.conf, never a field value. The page fills its
        fields once, from /setup/api/state."""
        loop = self._loopback()
        if not loop and not lan_allowed():
            self._send_json(403, {"error": "setup is not reachable over the network right now"})
            return
        if not self._authorized():
            self._send_json(200, {"need_pairing": True})
            return
        resp = {"need_pairing": False, "revision": tcfg_rev()}
        if loop:
            code, code_at = PAIRING.current_code()
            resp["pairing_code"] = code
            resp["pairing_code_remaining"] = max(0, int(WINDOW - (time.monotonic() - code_at)))
        self._send_json(200, resp)

    def _api_pair(self, data):
        if self._loopback():
            self._send_json(200, {"ok": True})
            return
        if not pairing_open():
            self._send_json(403, {"error": "the pairing code works only while the panel is not configured "
                                           "or the setup window is open"})
            return
        ip = self.client_address[0]
        if not PAIRING.allow_attempt(ip):
            self._send_json(429, {"error": "too many attempts; wait a few minutes"})
            return
        code = str(data.get("code", "")).strip()
        if not PAIRING.check(code):
            self._send_json(403, {"error": "wrong or expired pairing code"})
            return
        token, rec = SESSIONS.new("pair", ip)
        self._send_json(200, {"ok": True}, set_cookie=[
            _cookie("tsxsetup", token, max_age=WINDOW),
            _cookie("tsxcsrf", rec["csrf"], http_only=False, max_age=WINDOW)])

    # ---- the login with Home Assistant ----
    def _login_start(self):
        if self._loopback():
            self._redirect("/setup")
            return
        if not login_available():
            self._send_html(403, login_page("The login with Home Assistant is not available",
                                            "This panel does not know the URL of its Home Assistant, or the login "
                                            "is turned off. Open Setup on the panel and use the pairing code."))
            return
        ip = self.client_address[0]
        if not LOGIN_START_IP.allow(ip) or not LOGIN_START_ALL.allow("all"):
            self._send_html(429, login_page("Too many logins", "Wait a few minutes, then try again."))
            return
        host = self.headers.get("Host") or ""
        if not _HOST_RE.fullmatch(host):
            self._send_html(400, login_page("Bad request", "The request has no valid Host header."))
            return
        ha = ha_url()
        client_id = "http://%s/" % host
        redirect_uri = client_id + "setup/auth/callback"
        state = secrets.token_urlsafe(32)
        _pending_put(state, {"ip": ip, "ha": ha, "client_id": client_id, "created": time.monotonic()})
        query = urllib.parse.urlencode({"response_type": "code", "client_id": client_id,
                                        "redirect_uri": redirect_uri, "state": state})
        # Lax: the browser sends this cookie on the return from Home Assistant
        # (a top-level navigation from another site). The session cookie is Strict.
        self._redirect(ha + "/auth/authorize?" + query,
                       [_cookie("tsxlogin", state, path="/setup/auth", max_age=PENDING_S, same_site="Lax")])

    def _login_callback(self, q):
        clear = [_cookie("tsxlogin", "", path="/setup/auth", max_age=0, same_site="Lax")]
        if self._loopback():
            self._redirect("/setup", clear)
            return
        ip = self.client_address[0]
        if not LOGIN_RETURN_IP.allow(ip):
            self._send_html(429, login_page("Too many logins", "Wait a few minutes, then try again."), clear)
            return
        if not login_available():
            self._send_html(403, login_page("The login with Home Assistant is not available",
                                            "Open Setup on the panel and use the pairing code."), clear)
            return
        state = (q.get("state") or [""])[0]
        if "error" in q:
            _pending_take(state)
            self._send_html(403, login_page("The login was canceled", "Home Assistant did not log you in."), clear)
            return
        code = (q.get("code") or [""])[0]
        cookie_state = self._cookie_value("tsxlogin") or ""
        rec = _pending_take(state) if state and cookie_state and hmac.compare_digest(state, cookie_state) else None
        if rec is None or rec["ip"] != ip or rec["ha"] != ha_url() or not code or len(code) > 512:
            self._send_html(400, login_page("The login did not work",
                                            "The login is old, or it started in another browser. Start it again."),
                            clear)
            return
        user, err = ha_login_finish(rec, code)
        if user is None:
            sys.stderr.write("tsx-setupd: login from %s failed: %s\n" % (ip, err))
            self._send_html(502, login_page("The login did not work", err or "Home Assistant did not answer."), clear)
            return
        if not user["is_admin"]:
            sys.stderr.write("tsx-setupd: login from %s refused: %s is not an administrator\n" % (ip, user["name"]))
            self._send_html(403, login_page("Only an administrator can open the setup page",
                                            "The Home Assistant user %s is not an administrator." % user["name"]),
                            clear)
            return
        token, srec = SESSIONS.new("ha", ip, user["name"], user["id"])
        sys.stderr.write("tsx-setupd: login: %s (Home Assistant administrator) from %s\n" % (user["name"], ip))
        self._send_html(200, login_page("Logged in", "You are logged in as %s." % user["name"], go=True), clear + [
            _cookie("tsxsetup", token, max_age=SESSION_S),
            _cookie("tsxcsrf", srec["csrf"], http_only=False, max_age=SESSION_S)])

    def _api_logout(self):
        token = self._session_token()
        if not self._loopback() and token:
            SESSIONS.drop(token)
        self._send_json(200, {"ok": True}, set_cookie=[
            _cookie("tsxsetup", "", max_age=0), _cookie("tsxcsrf", "", http_only=False, max_age=0)])

    def _api_sessions(self):
        if not self._authorized():
            self._send_json(403, {"error": "not authorized"})
            return
        cur = None if self._loopback() else self._session_token()
        self._send_json(200, {"sessions": SESSIONS.listing(cur)})

    def _api_sessions_revoke(self, data):
        if not self._authorized():
            self._send_json(403, {"error": "not authorized"})
            return
        if data.get("all") is True:
            n = SESSIONS.drop_all()
            sys.stderr.write("tsx-setupd: all %d sessions revoked\n" % n)
            self._send_json(200, {"ok": True, "revoked": n})
            return
        sid = data.get("id")
        if not isinstance(sid, str) or not sid:
            self._send_json(400, {"error": "the request needs a session id or all"})
            return
        if not SESSIONS.drop_id(sid):
            self._send_json(404, {"error": "no such session"})
            return
        sys.stderr.write("tsx-setupd: session %s revoked\n" % sid)
        self._send_json(200, {"ok": True, "revoked": 1})

    # ---- the Panel section ----
    def _api_panel_get(self):
        if not self._authorized():
            self._send_json(403, {"error": "not authorized"})
            return
        obj, err = panel_state()
        if obj is None:
            self._send_json(502, {"error": err})
            return
        self._send_json(200, obj)

    def _api_panel_post(self, data):
        if not self._authorized():
            self._send_json(403, {"error": "not authorized"})
            return
        control = data.get("control")
        if not isinstance(control, str):
            self._send_json(400, {"error": "the request needs a control"})
            return
        code, obj = panel_set(control, data.get("value"))
        self._send_json(code, obj)

    def _api_learn_reset(self):
        """Forget the brightness points that the panel learned."""
        if not self._authorized():
            self._send_json(403, {"error": "not authorized"})
            return
        ok, text = helper_call("brightness-learn-reset")
        if ok:
            self._send_json(200, {"ok": True})
        else:
            self._send_json(502, {"error": text or "could not forget the learned brightness"})

    def _api_submit(self, data):
        if not self._authorized():
            self._send_json(403, {"error": "not authorized"})
            return
        # {"revision": "...", "fields": {KEY: VALUE}}: the fields that the
        # user changed, and the revision of panel.conf that the page loaded
        fields = data.get("fields")
        if not isinstance(fields, dict):
            self._send_json(400, {"errors": {"_request": "the request has no fields. Reload the page."}})
            return
        fields = {k: v for k, v in fields.items() if isinstance(k, str)}
        code, obj = handle_submit(fields, data.get("revision"), on_screen=self._on_screen())
        self._send_json(code, obj)


# ---- the page itself (static. All state comes from the JSON API) -------

PAGE_UNAVAILABLE = """<!doctype html>
<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Panel setup</title>
<style>
  :root { --bg:#0b0e12; --fg:#e8ecf1; --muted:#8b96a3; }
  body { background:var(--bg); color:var(--fg); font-family:system-ui,sans-serif;
         display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:center; height:100vh; margin:0; }
  .box { max-width:640px; padding:32px; text-align:center; }
  h1 { font-size:1.4rem; }
  p { color:var(--muted); line-height:1.5; }
</style></head>
<body><div class="box">
  <h1>Setup is not available over the network right now</h1>
  <p>This panel is already configured, and it has no Home Assistant URL for
  the login with Home Assistant. Open <b>Setup</b> on the panel itself (the
  quick-settings overlay, or <code>tsx-config setup</code> over ssh) to allow
  the pairing code for about 15 minutes.</p>
</div></body></html>
"""

PAGE_MAIN = """<!doctype html>
